 "class_note": "The confusion classes are built from Azure's IPA phoneme symbols, and only en-US returns them named. The single en-GB pass run from 2026-09-14 to 2026-09-16 returned the scores and the n-best slots but every symbol EMPTY, so i/ii, schwa, s/z, cat/cut and j/y lost their source and the spelling-based fallback (classify_word) carried them alone. The counts below are therefore mixed: the 09-10 read contributes real phoneme evidence, the other five only spelling guesses. phonemes_before_2026_09_16 holds the old two-pass tallies. Treat class weights as provisional until en-US reads accumulate.",
 "locale_note": "SCORING LOCALE CHANGED TO en-US ON 2026-09-16, at the user's decision. Microsoft's docs make en-US the only locale that returns prosody AND named IPA phonemes. MEASURED, not assumed: the 2026-09-10 read (B003-B006) was re-run under en-US on the SAME audio, the SAME Whisper transcript and the SAME 749-word reference. Only the locale changed, and it moved prosody 52.0 -> 81.4, pron 76.9 -> 87.9, fluency 89.7 -> 93.6, accuracy 93.5 -> 90.3, completeness 94.9 -> 93.2. Phoneme names went from 0 of 55 to 70 of 70, with n-best alternatives. So en-GB and en-US rows are DIFFERENT SERIES and every read row now carries `locale`. Never draw one line through both. The five rows of 09-12 to 09-16 are still en-GB; whether to spend ~188 more minutes of Azure rescoring them into one consistent series is the user's call.",
